To check if a number is a power of three without using loops or recursion, we can use the bit manipulation technique. The idea is to keep shifting the bits of the number by one position and check if the number is divisible by three after each shift. If at any point the number becomes zero or the shift count exceeds the log base three of the maximum possible number, then the number is not a power of three.

Here's the Java code for the same:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    int power = 1;
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 3 != 0) return false;
        power *= 3;
        n /= 3;
    }
    return n == power;
}

// Optimized version using bit manipulation
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    return (Long.valueOf(1L << (32 * Integer.SIZE - 2)) & n) == n && Integer.bitCount(n) % 2 == 0;
}
```

The first method checks if the number is a power of three by repeatedly dividing it by three and checking the remainder. The second method uses bit manipulation to check if the number is a power of three. It checks if the last bit of the number is set and if the number of set bits is even. This is because a power of three has a 1 in its last bit and has an even number of 1s in its binary representation.

