In Java, the `==` operator is used to compare the reference of two objects, not their values. When you use `==` to compare strings, Java is actually checking if the two strings are the same object in memory, not if they have the same value.

On the other hand, the `equals()` method is used to compare the values of two strings. When you call `.equals()` on a string object, Java compares the sequence of characters in the two strings, starting from the first character, until it finds a difference or reaches the end of both strings.

