To find the common elements in two arrays without using any extra data structures, you can use the following approach:

1. Initialize an empty hash table or an object to store the elements of the first array as keys.
2. Iterate through the second array and for each element, check if it is present in the hash table.
3. If the element is present, then it is a common element, print it and remove it from the hash table to avoid duplicates.

Here is the implementation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function findCommonElements(arr1, arr2) {
  let map = {};
  let result = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
    if (map[arr1[i]] !== undefined) {
      result.push(arr1[i]);
      delete map[arr1[i]];
    } else {
      map[arr1[i]] = true;
    }
  }

  for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {
    if (map[arr2[j]]) {
      result.push(arr2[j]);
      delete map[arr2[j]];
      break;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

This function takes two arrays as arguments and returns an array of common elements. The time complexity of this solution is O(n + m), where n and m are the lengths of the input arrays.

Note that this solution assumes that the arrays do not contain duplicate elements. If the arrays can contain duplicates, you will need to modify the solution to handle that case appropriately.

